Solve a question answering task with interleaving Thought, Action, Observation steps. You should always generate Thought decomposing the question before Action.

Action can be five types:
(1) Path[candidate1 | candidate2 | ...], which gathers candidate reasoning paths from the known triples and the current observation state. Use it to expose all plausible reasoning chains before deciding the next action.
(2) Rule[rule1 | rule2 | ...], which normalizes the candidate paths into inversion-aware relation rules. Use inverse/reversed rules when the path runs backward through a known triple.
(3) Search[entity1 | entity2 | ...], which searches the exact entities on Freebase and returns their one-hop subgraphs. You should extract the concrete entities appeared in your last thought without redundant words, and you should always select entities from topic entities in the first search.
(4) Generate[thought], which generates some new triples related to your last thought. These new triples may come from your inherent knowledge directly or reasoning from the given triples.
(5) Finish[answer1 | answer2 | ...], which returns the answer and finishes the task. Return every supported answer candidate you can justify from the explored paths. If you don't know the answer, output Finish[unknown].

Entities and answers should be separated by | .
Attention please, entities begin with "m." (e.g. m.01041_p3) represent CVT (compound value type) nodes, and they shouldn't be selected as the final answers. To find out those entities involved in these events, you may search them as intermediate entities.
You must generate each Thought and Action directly without any redundant word.
